# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
#
# gpon/Kconfig -- the hardware-decoupled GPON protocol core, common to every
# OpenWrt GPON target in this tree.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   The Kconfig half of the build wiring for drivers/net/gpon/: the one symbol
#   a vendor GPON driver selects to pull in the shared PLOAM / OMCI / GEM
#   protocol core.  It declares no driver of its own -- nothing here probes a
#   device, and there is no hardware called "GPON core".
#
# WHY IT IS COMMON -- and which targets and architectures compile it
#   Operator, 2026-08-05: "en openwrt deberia estar estructurado algo asi:
#   rtl960x* para la familia para tener codigo comun" ... "la idea es poner en
#   comun el codigo que corresponde para no tener mucho duplicado", and on the
#   two per-target monoliths that each carried a private copy of the protocol:
#   "mal, poner en comun".
#   G.984/G.988 are specifications, not chip facts, so exactly ONE copy of them
#   may exist in the tree.  This file is authored once, in the shared files-
#   tree target/linux/gpon-common/files-6.18/, and reaches both targets through
#   FILES_DIR (see their target Makefiles).  Compiled by:
#     - realtek-elnath  RTL9607F (Cortina)  aarch64, LITTLE-endian
#     - realtek-luna    RTL960xC (Luna)     MIPS32,  BIG-endian
#     - dev/rtl9607c-test on x86-64 through fuzz_shims/, where the same sources
#       are fuzzed under ASan+UBSan without a board
#   The prefix is gpon_ and not rtl960x_ on purpose: the layer must also serve
#   the future ARM OLT and other brands, so a Realtek-named prefix would be too
#   narrow for what it covers.
#
# THE CORE/SHELL RULE IT OBEYS
#   Everything built from drivers/net/gpon/ is FUNCTIONAL CORE: it decides, it
#   never does.  It parses wire bytes, runs the state machines and builds reply
#   buffers; the per-target shell (drivers/net/ethernet/{realtek,cortina}/)
#   reads and writes registers, arms DMA, services interrupts and calls the
#   core through an op table.
#   ==> NO OBJECT WIRED HERE MAY EVER GAIN AN MMIO ACCESS.  No readl/writel, no
#   ioremap, no device pointer, no sleeping, no kernel timers -- time is an
#   explicit input.  A file that needs any of those belongs in the target's own
#   files- tree, not in this directory.  gpon_core_purity_test in
#   dev/rtl9607c-test/ is the re-runnable proof, not this comment.
#
# NOT A PATCH, BY HARD RULE
#   Driver wiring lives in a browsable source file under files-<ver>/, never in
#   a patches-<ver>/*.patch -- not even for Kconfig or Makefile.  An AI-authored
#   patch is un-findable by the next human or LLM, and editing one keeps
#   silently re-breaking the source it applies to.

config GPON_CORE
	bool
	help
	  The hardware-decoupled GPON protocol core shared by every GPON target
	  in this tree: the PLOAM activation state machine, the ITU-T G.988 OMCI
	  responder and the GEM/T-CONT provisioning lifecycle.

	  There is nothing to choose here.  A vendor GPON driver that wants the
	  shared core selects this symbol; no target's config-<ver> mentions it,
	  so the "enabled it for one target and forgot the other" failure mode
	  cannot occur.

	  It is deliberately a bool and not a tristate: both GPON drivers in this
	  tree ship built in (CONFIG_CORTINA_GPON=y, CONFIG_RTL9602C_GPON=y), an
	  ONU has <=64 MB RAM and a small NAND, and a separate gpon_core.ko would
	  cost module-loader overhead and a MODULE_LICENSE translation unit while
	  buying nothing.  Built in also means the core's entry points are plain
	  globals -- correct as long as every consumer is built in too.  A vendor
	  driver set to =m would fail modpost with "undefined!" on those symbols;
	  the fix then is EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL in the core, not a change here.
